{"author":{"id":"8d7a7fea5b07a0d5d081edd516d55afeb51ce9c18b2b2be6ceb30eb17cd9b547"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794290251,"nonce":"y3BZlBhds7ZKT39Fj5IWvWw2kg0=","title":"incremental_update compares and rewrites the whole tree for every change","message":"Goal (owner): only what changes is recomputed. Today a one-colour change makes the builder rerun, then incremental_update hashes the whole new element tree, walks every node (analyze_changes, then update_render_props_in_place for each node: compute_element, compute_element_tree and compute_topology_tree again, render_props() built twice) and replaces props and taffy style of every node. The work follows the tree, not the change. Evidence: git-bug 22e0b64 (Tasks click: incremental_update 5 ms of a 10.5 ms frame; builder 0.17 ms) and the PR 71 review (17.6 ms at 3003 nodes).\nDone so far on our side (in PR 71 and PR 72): a node whose own hash is unchanged keeps its props and its taffy style, and restyle follows only the replaced nodes (restyle cost went from 3x the update to 1.05x). The walk and the hashing remain.\nProposal for Darmie (no PR before agreement): (1) hash each element once per update and keep the per-node hashes, not three hashes per node; (2) skip a whole subtree when its stored tree hash equals the new one (the node_hashes already hold it), so an unchanged subtree costs one comparison, not a walk; (3) let Stateful components, which know what changed, update their own nodes without the global rebuild.","files":null}]}